\subsection{Generalization}
Recombination (A, B, E) and size (C2) are harder for the reactive agent only; longer chains (C) cost the learned agents about @{GAP('macro_gru','C_chain_length')!.2f} (Fig.~\ref{fig:main}b). The decisive regime is D: with @{NM('macro_gru','D_rule_permutation')} runs, Macro-GRU scores @{TE('macro_gru','D_rule_permutation','train_heldout')!.3f} on its training distribution but @{TE('macro_gru','D_rule_permutation')!.3f} on unseen colors (Macro-FF @{TE('macro_ff','D_rule_permutation')!.3f}), while the tracker is unaffected (@{TE('hypothesis','D_rule_permutation')!.3f}). The belief-augmented agent reaches @{TE('macro_gruB','D_rule_permutation')!.3f}, indistinguishable from the tracker (@{PS('D: Hypothesis vs Macro-GRU+belief')}) and far above the plain agent (@{PS('D: Macro-GRU+belief vs Macro-GRU')}): its inputs do not mention colors, so the failure is one of symbol-specific policies. Regimes B, C, E use @{NM('macro_gru','B_compositional')} runs and A, C2 use @{NM('macro_gru','A_interpolation')}; those cells are indicative.

\subsection{What matters inside the tracker}
In a reward-form $\times$ information-bonus ablation at difficulty 5, raising $\lambda$ from 0 to 8 lifts success from @{FX('hyp_EV_l0',5)!.2f} to @{FX('hyp_EV_l8',5)!.2f} with expected-value reward and from @{FX('psrl_S_l0',5)!.2f} to @{FX('psrl_S_l8',5)!.2f} with sampled reward (Fig.~\ref{fig:second}d). Memory and revision are necessary (difficulty 4: @{A('full_memory')!.2f} with memory, @{A('no_memory')!.2f} without; @{A('revision_on')!.2f} with revision, @{A('revision_off')!.2f} without); the bonus matters only where distractors make uncertainty costly.

\subsection{False assumptions, noise and the vault}
The tracker's exclusivity assumption is catastrophic when false: when one key opens several doors it succeeds in @{MK('hypothesis','master_key_d3')!.2f} (difficulty 3) and @{MK('hypothesis','master_key_d4')!.2f} (difficulty 4) of episodes, against @{MK('hyp_nonexcl','master_key_d3')!.2f} and @{MK('hyp_nonexcl','master_key_d4')!.2f} without the assumption, which in turn costs about @{MK('hypothesis','exclusive_control_d4')-MK('hyp_nonexcl','exclusive_control_d4')!.2f} when it is true. The soft prior recovers @{MK('hyp_adaptive','master_key_d3')!.2f} and @{MK('hyp_adaptive','master_key_d4')!.2f} and matches the hard rule on the exclusive control (@{PS('exclusive_control_d4: Hyp.-adaptive vs Hypothesis')}); this repairs a \emph{detectable} violation only (Fig.~\ref{fig:second}c). With jammed locks, PSRL drops from @{SRN('psrl',3,0.0)!.2f} to @{SRN('psrl',3,0.4)!.2f} at $\epsilon=0.4$ and difficulty 3 while the tracker falls from @{SRN('hypothesis',3,0.0)!.2f} to @{SRN('hypothesis',3,0.4)!.2f} (@{PS('slip 0.4, d=3: Hypothesis vs PSRL')}); the noise-free-trained learned agents reach @{SRN('macro_gru',3,0.4)!.2f} (Macro-GRU) and @{SRN('macro_gruB',3,0.4)!.2f} (with beliefs). At difficulty 4 all agents lose a similar amount, so this is not a general advantage of explicit testing.

In the vault the elimination agent opens every code (@{VT('hypothesis','in_dist')!.2f}, @{VT('hypothesis','larger')!.2f}; 95\% failure-rate bound @{RO3(900)!.2%} for 900 episodes) with @{VT('hypothesis','in_dist','resets')!.2f} and @{VT('hypothesis','larger','resets')!.2f} failed presses against @{VT('hypothesis','in_dist','pred_elim_resets')!.2f} and @{VT('hypothesis','larger','pred_elim_resets')!.2f} predicted by Proposition~\ref{prop:cost}; on the grid, single-gate environments give @{TH('hypothesis',4)!.3f} failed door tests at difficulty 4 against @{TH('hypothesis',4,'pred_elim')!.3f} predicted. PPO-GRU solves small codes (@{VT('vault_gru','in_dist')!.2f}) but only @{VT('vault_gru','larger')!.2f} of larger ones over @{NM('vault_gru')} runs (@{PS('vault larger codes: Hypothesis vs PPO-GRU')}).


\subsection{External check: CausalGame}
On the survival part of CausalGame~\cite{chen2026causalgame} (14 scenarios, 40 sessions per agent and scenario; unit: scenario) every search agent beats the default design (EV+IG against default: $\Delta=@{CGD(3,'diff')!+.3f}$, $p_{\mathrm{Holm}}=@{CGD(3,'p_holm')!.3f}$), but the EV+IG agent, which uses the same Bayesian model as posterior sampling and picks experiments by expected survival plus an information bonus, is not better than the simpler ones (Table~\ref{tab:res}(c)); the bonus makes no detectable difference. No hypothesis space matching the generator is supplied here, which is consistent with Sect.~\ref{sec:belief}: the benefit of explicit hypotheses depends on a hypothesis space that fits the environment. The benchmark's LLM-judged report rubric cannot be applied to these agents, and our numbers are not comparable to its leaderboard.

